            <ArticleTitle>Spontaneous rupture of splenic hematoma in a malaria patient: Case report and review of literature.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>241-244</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S2210-2612(16)30414-X</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ijscr.2016.10.017</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Blunt abdominal trauma is the most common cause of splenic rupture. Malaria is the most frequent tropical infectious cause of spontaneous splenic rupture. The exact mechanism is not well-defined.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE REPORT" NlmCategory="METHODS">We report a case of thirty-year-old male patient known to have malaria who presented with spontaneous splenic rupture. A trial of conservative treatment failed and splenecomy was done to control bleeding.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Spontaneous splenic rupture should be kept in mind in malaria patients presenting with left upper quadrant pain and signs of hypovolemia. Early diagnosis and treatment is essential.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Copyright © 2016. Published by Elsevier Ltd.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
